 ## Overview
-This method implements direct volume ray casting on programmable graphics hardware (specifically DirectX 9-level architectures like the ATI 9700) using a multi-pass stream model [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of CPU-based volume rendering by exploiting the intrinsic parallelism of fragment processors and efficient inter-pass communication via texture render targets [S1, unofficial]. Unlike single-pass rasterization, this approach iteratively accumulates optical properties across multiple rendering passes, enabling early ray termination and empty-space skipping through hardware-supported depth testing [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Direct volume rendering](1184-direct-volume-rendering.md) in its explicit multi-pass accumulation strategy and from [Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering](5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md) by its specific reliance on the "stream model" for managing ray state between passes.
+This method implements direct volume ray casting on programmable graphics hardware (specifically DirectX 9-level architectures like the ATI 9700) using a multi-pass stream model [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of integrating early ray termination and empty-space skipping into 3D texture-based rendering by exploiting intrinsic parallelism and efficient communication between rendering passes [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from single-pass hardware approaches by its use of a feedback loop where the render target of one pass serves as the input texture for the next, allowing the accumulation state to be checked and modified between steps [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates as a multi-pass pipeline where the output of one pass serves as the input texture for the next. It relies on specific hardware features: Pixel Shader 2.0, per-fragment texture fetches (up to 8 textures, limited dependent fetches), texture render targets (allowing rendering to 2D textures aligned with the viewport), texture coordinate generation, per-fragment arithmetic, and depth replace capabilities [S1, unofficial].
+The method uses a multi-pass approach where ray traversal is split across several rendering passes. The core mechanism relies on the "early z-test" to discard fragments that have already met the termination criteria, thereby skipping unnecessary shader execution for subsequent passes [S1, unofficial].
 
